.TH SPACES 8 "2026-10-07" "storage-spaces 1.1.1" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
spaces \- read, write and manage Microsoft Storage Spaces pools on Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RB [ \-\-unclean\-parity
.IR refuse | data ]
.I command
.PP
.B spaces scan
.br
.B spaces attach
.RB [ \-\-pool
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-space
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-backend
.IR auto | dm | ublk | nbd | fuse ]
.RB [ \-\-degraded ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RB [ \-\-rw ]
.RI [ device ...]
.br
.B spaces detach
.RI [ space ]
.br
.B spaces status
.br
.B spaces info
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.IR device ...
.br
.B spaces extents
.I device ...
.B \-\-space
.I name
.br
.B spaces export
.I device ...
.B \-\-space
.I name
.B \-\-output
.I file
.br
.B spaces dm\-table
.I device ...
.B \-\-space
.I name
.br
.B spaces dump
.I device ...
.br
.B spaces diff \-\-old
.I device ...
.B \-\-new
.I device ...
.br
.B spaces pool
.BR create | rename | repair | optimize | scrub | health | remove
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.I device ...
.br
.B spaces space
.BR create | delete | rename | resize
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.I device ...
.br
.B spaces tier create
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.I device ...
.br
.B spaces disk
.BR add | set | retire | remove
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.I device ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B spaces
reads the metadata of Microsoft Storage Spaces pools created by Windows 11
(pool versions 28 and 29) from their member disks and exposes the virtual
disks ("spaces") as Linux block devices, read-only unless a space is
attached with
.BR \-\-rw .
The
.BR pool ,
.B space
and
.B disk
commands create and change pools as Windows 11 24H2 does; they write only
with
.BR \-\-yes .
Pool members are never written otherwise.
.PP
Supported: simple, two- and three-way mirror, single and dual parity spaces
(including the local reconstruction code of 11 and more columns), storage
tiers and mirror-accelerated parity, fixed and thin provisioning, several
spaces per pool, the per-space write-back cache, 512e and 4Kn member disks,
pools with missing, failing or retired disks as far as the redundancy
allows, and pools after a crash. Writing: simple, mirror and single parity
spaces of clean pools, fixed and thin (not dual parity, storage tiers,
degraded pools, or rows of thin spaces with other allocation units than
256 MiB that are not allocated yet). Management: creating pools (version
28) and simple, mirror and single parity spaces, fixed and thin; deleting,
renaming and growing spaces; adding, retiring, removing and replacing
disks; tier templates and tiered spaces (an SSD mirror over an HDD simple
or parity tier); repair, optimize, scrub and a health report in Windows'
terms. Not supported: creating dual parity spaces, writing tiered spaces,
new spaces in pools of version 29, pools created by other Windows releases.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B scan
Lists the Storage Spaces pool members found among the block devices, grouped
by pool, with the spaces of each pool (as root; without, it says how many
devices it could not open).
.TP
.B attach
Attaches the spaces of every complete pool (or of the selected pool/space).
Each space appears as
.BI /dev/mapper/ss\- pool \- space
and every partition inside it as
.BI /dev/mapper/ss\- pool \- space \-p N\fR;
when a space of another pool of the same name has that name already, the
first eight hex digits of the pool's GUID follow the space's name.
Pools with missing disks are skipped unless
.B \-\-degraded
is given; pools with fewer than half of their disks are skipped unless
.B \-\-force
is given too (the disks at hand may hold an old state of the pool).
Spaces already attached are left alone.
With
.B \-\-rw
the spaces are attached read-write, if their state is fully understood
(clean pool, healthy space, a supported resiliency); otherwise the reason is
printed. Mirror spaces keep their dirty region log, single parity spaces are
written through their write-back cache and parity journal, as Windows does.
Thin spaces allocate rows as writes first reach them; discards give rows of
thin simple and mirror spaces back to the pool once they cover them whole.
Writes are durable once flushed or after
.BR detach .
Not with
.B \-\-degraded
or
.BR \-\-force .
.TP
.B detach
Removes the devices of the given space (name, GUID or device-mapper name) or
of all attached spaces, and stops their serving processes.
.TP
.B status
Lists attached spaces, their backends and partition devices (also for users
other than root).
.TP
.B info\fR, \fBextents\fR, \fBexport\fR, \fBdm\-table
Inspect a pool given by its member devices or images: pool, disks (usage,
media type, missing) and spaces (layout, provisioning, and whether they are
healthy, degraded or failed with the disks at hand, plus the state of the
parity journal, dirty region tracking and write-back cache); the slab
allocation of a space; copy a space into a sparse image file; print a
device-mapper table for a simple or mirror space.
.TP
.B dump\fR, \fBdiff
Print the pool metadata one fact per line (every copy of the pool
database, its records raw and decoded, the dirty region log, parity journal
and write-back cache of each space), or the lines only one of two states of
a pool has, for comparing a pool before and after a change.
.SH MANAGEMENT
Every
.BR pool ,
.B space
and
.B disk
command takes the pool's disks (block devices or image files), prints the
plan of what it would write and writes it only with
.BR \-\-yes ,
in steps that each end with a flush, so that a crash leaves a pool that
opens in the state before or after a step. The disks are opened
exclusively (O_EXCL, a lock on image files): detach the pool first. Only
clean pools are changed (every disk present, no out-of-date copy, every
record understood), except that
.BR "pool repair" ,
.B disk add
and
.B disk remove
accept missing disks, to replace them. Disks are named by their ids as
.B info
lists them.
.TP
.B pool create \-\-name \fIname\fR [\-\-logical\-sector 512|4096] [\-\-wipe]
A pool of the given disks, which must be blank (nothing in their first and
last MiB) unless
.B \-\-wipe
is given, which destroys what they hold.
.TP
.B pool rename \-\-name \fIname\fR, \fBpool remove
Rename the pool; remove a pool without spaces (its partitions leave the
disks' partition tables).
.TP
.B pool repair
Rebuild every copy on a missing disk or out of date on another disk, from a
current mirror copy or the other parity columns.
.TP
.B pool optimize
Spread the extents evenly over the disks, as after adding one.
.TP
.B pool scrub [\-\-repair]
Compare every copy of the mirror spaces and every stripe of the single
parity spaces; differences where writes were under way are counted apart.
.B \-\-repair
makes everything agree (the first mirror copy, parity from the data).
.TP
.B pool health
The HealthStatus and OperationalStatus Windows would show for the pool, its
disks and spaces with the disks given, and the disk failures each space
still survives.
.TP
.B space create \-\-name \fIname\fR \-\-resiliency simple|mirror|parity \-\-size \fIsize\fR
With
.BR \-\-thin ,
.BI \-\-copies " 2|3" ,
.BI \-\-columns " n" ,
.BI \-\-interleave " size" ,
.BI \-\-write\-cache " size"
(parity); Windows' defaults otherwise. Sizes such as 10G are rounded up to
whole rows.
.TP
.B space create \-\-name \fIname\fR \-\-tier \fItemplate\fB=\fIsize\fR \-\-tier \fItemplate\fB=\fIsize
A tiered space: an SSD tier, then an HDD tier, each from a template, with
its cache and logs on the SSD disks.
.TP
.B tier create \-\-name \fIname\fR \-\-media ssd|hdd \-\-resiliency simple|mirror|parity [\-\-columns \fIn\fR]
A tier template (New-StorageTier); the disks' media come from
.BR "disk set \-\-media" .
.TP
.B space delete\fR|\fBrename\fR|\fBresize \-\-space \fIname\fR
Delete a space and its data, rename it
.RB ( \-\-name ),
or grow it
.RB ( \-\-size ;
the file system inside is not grown).
.TP
.B disk add \-\-new \fIdevice\fR [\-\-wipe]
Add a blank disk to the pool of the other devices (at most four).
.TP
.B disk set \-\-disk \fIid\fR [\-\-media unspecified|hdd|ssd] [\-\-usage auto\-select|manual\-select|hot\-spare]
Change a disk's media type or usage.
.TP
.B disk retire \-\-disk \fIid\fR, \fBdisk remove \-\-disk \fIid
Move everything off a disk; remove a disk that holds nothing any more, or a
missing one after a repair.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-unclean\-parity " " refuse | data
After an unclean shutdown (crash, power loss, disks pulled), parity
stripes, mirror rows and write-back cache slots with writes in flight can
be inconsistent. Windows repairs parity stripes when it mounts the pool,
and which side it keeps is not recorded; mirror copies it never reconciles
and reads either one. By default reads of parity stripes whose parity does
not match, of mirror rows whose copies differ and of write-back cache
chunks whose mirrored copies disagree fail with an I/O error;
.B data
returns the data as it is on disk (the highest mirror copy, the newest
cache and journal entries). Attaching the pool to Windows once repairs its
parity stripes.
.SH BACKENDS
.TP
.B dm
Device-mapper table over the member devices; no process. Used by
.B auto
for simple spaces whose logical sector size matches the members' (read-write
only when the space is fully allocated and its write-back cache is empty).
.TP
.B ublk
A
.B spaces serve\-ublk
process (transient systemd unit) behind
.BR /dev/ublkb N;
needs Linux 6.0 or newer and the
.B ublk_drv
module. Exposes the space's own sector size and keeps reading when a mirror
copy or parity column fails.
.TP
.B nbd
A
.B spaces serve\-nbd
process connected with
.BR nbd\-client (8).
.TP
.B fuse
A
.B spaces serve\-fuse
process exposing an image file, attached with
.BR losetup (8).
Read-only.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/storage-spaces/
State of attached spaces.
.TP
.I /etc/udev/rules.d/69-storage-spaces.rules
Starts
.B storage-spaces-attach.service
when a pool member appears.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
spaces scan
spaces attach
mount \-o ro /dev/mapper/ss\-Storage_pool\-Data\-p2 /mnt
spaces detach
spaces attach \-\-rw \-\-space Data
.PP
spaces pool create \-\-name Data \-\-yes /dev/sdb /dev/sdc /dev/sdd
spaces space create \-\-name Files \-\-resiliency mirror \-\-size 1T \\
    \-\-yes /dev/sdb /dev/sdc /dev/sdd
spaces pool health /dev/sdb /dev/sdc /dev/sdd
spaces disk add \-\-new /dev/sde \-\-yes /dev/sdb /dev/sdc /dev/sdd
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dmsetup (8),
.BR nbd\-client (8),
.BR losetup (8),
.BR mount (8)
